Loaded playbook: web_discovery
Description: First-pass discovery of a web application's attack surface: technology, endpoints, parameters, authentication mechanisms, and everything shipped to the client. Produces the inventory that the per-category playbooks are assessed against. Finds surface; does not attempt exploitation. 
Mode: crew

╭─────────────────────────────────────── Starting ───────────────────────────────────────╮
│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                    │
│                                                                                        │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                          │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                        │
│ Mode: Crew                                                                             │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                         │
│ Max loops: 50                                                                          │
│ Task: First-pass discovery of a web application's attack surface: technology,          │
│ endpoints, parameters, authentication mechanisms, and everything shipped to the        │
│ client. Produces the inventory that the per-category playbooks are assessed against.   │
│ Finds surface; does not attempt exploitation.                                          │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Fingerprint the stack                                                           │
│ Objective: Identify what is running so later playbooks can be selected sensibly.       │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Identify server, framework and client technology from headers, cookies, error     │
│ pages, asset names and response shapes.                                                │
│   2. Note the authentication mechanism and session representation (cookie names, token │
│ formats, headers).                                                                     │
│   3. Record the stack explicitly: it determines which stack- specific playbook should  │
│ run next (for example a client-side .NET or SPA bundle, or a particular reverse        │
│ proxy).                                                                                │
│   4. Identify API style and locate any metadata, schema or documentation endpoint      │
│ (OpenAPI, GraphQL introspection, framework metadata).                                  │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Enumerate the surface                                                           │
│ Objective: Map endpoints, parameters and inputs.                                       │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Enumerate routes and endpoints from metadata documents, client code, sitemaps and │
│ directory discovery.                                                                   │
│   2. Record every parameter and input point, including headers and cookies, and which  │
│ are reflected in responses.                                                            │
│   3. For each endpoint note whether it responds without authentication, and what       │
│ differs when authenticated. This baseline is what later control tests compare against  │
│ - capture it carefully.                                                                │
│   4. Note any endpoint that dispatches, batches, proxies or forwards requests          │
│ internally, as these bypass controls applied to direct routes.                         │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Collect the client payload                                                      │
│ Objective: Everything the browser receives is public and must be retrieved.            │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Download what the client downloads: scripts, bundles, source maps, compiled       │
│ binaries, WebAssembly, configuration blobs.                                            │
│   2. Compiled or minified client code must be unpacked or decompiled before it can be  │
│ searched. A clean search over a binary or a minified bundle is NOT evidence of         │
│ absence.                                                                               │
│   3. Record where artifacts were written so later playbooks reuse them rather than     │
│ downloading again.                                                                     │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Write the inventory                                                             │
│ Objective: Leave a durable, structured record.                                         │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Save to notes: technology stack, endpoints with auth status, parameters, client   │
│ artifacts and their paths, and any credential-bearing material observed.               │
│   2. Do not attempt exploitation here. Flag candidates for the category playbooks      │
│ instead, with the evidence that makes them candidates.                                 │
│                                                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
